"Stats & Probability Revision May 2026 page 2; Revision 2 June 2026 page 11; both visually checked", "review_note": "Restored the missing twentieth score, 12, and checked the count directly.", "release": true, "fields": {"stem": "The chemistry scores of 20 students are 8, 9, 10, 7, 9; 8, 12, 7, 6, 16; 7, 6, 11, 5, 13; 13, 8, 13, 8, 12. If x% of the students scored at most 8, what is 2x?"}, "explanation": "There are ten scores at most 8: four 8s, three 7s, two 6s, and one 5. Thus x = (10/20)×100 = 50, so 2x = 100.", "correct": "B"}, {"id": "3273c3c8-0d1c-baa4-34fc-e849e60cefab", "before": {"stem": "Here are some values from a chemistry exam for 20\nstudents. (calc)\n8 9 10 7 9\n8 12 7 6 16\n’; © H&K\nI3 8 13 8 12\nIf the number who get 8 or below represents x%.\nWhat is the value of 2x.", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "60"}, {"key": "B", "text": "100"}, {"key": "C", "text": "30"}, {"key": "D", "text": "50"}], "correct": "B", "explanation": "Independently solved and checked against the original scanned page and printed choices."}, "source_question_id": "revision_2_june_2026_a-p11-q8", "source": "Stats & Probability Revision May 2026 page 2; Revision 2 June 2026 page 11; both visually checked", "review_note": "Restored the missing twentieth score, 12, and checked the count directly.", "release": true, "fields": {"stem": "The chemistry scores of 20 students are 8, 9, 10, 7, 9; 8, 12, 7, 6, 16; 7, 6, 11, 5, 13; 13, 8, 13, 8, 12. If x% of the students scored at most 8, what is 2x?"}, "explanation": "There are ten scores at most 8: four 8s, three 7s, two 6s, and one 5. Thus x = (10/20)×100 = 50, so 2x = 100.", "correct": "B"}, {"id": "5f813fc7-c4f2-0370-2f8e-69fb9b511e02", "before": {"stem": "3,20,27.44,60,61\nWhat is the interquartile range for the\nvalues above? (no calc)", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "59"}, {"key": "B", "text": "20"}, {"key": "C", "text": "60"}, {"key": "D", "text": "40"}], "correct": "D", "explanation": "Independently solved and checked against the original scanned page and printed choices."}, "source_question_id": "revision_2_june_2026_a-p12-q10", "source": "Revision 2 June 2026, rendered page 12", "review_note": "Restored the comma between 27 and 44; stated the median-of-halves quartile convention explicitly.", "release": true, "fields": {"stem": "Find the interquartile range of 3, 20, 27, 44, 60, 61, using the medians of the lower and upper halves as Q1 and Q3."}, "explanation": "The lower half is 3, 20, 27, whose median is Q1 = 20. The upper half is 44, 60, 61, whose median is Q3 = 60. The interquartile range is Q3 − Q1 = 60 − 20 = 40.", "correct": "D"}, {"id": "8896aba3-a121-8529-4df4-f8d3a7dcee7c", "before": {"stem": "Q72. The graph plotted below represents which of the following functions? Abdelrahman", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "f(x) = (x + 1)(x − 2)²"}, {"key": "B", "text": "f(x) = (x − 1)(x + 2)²"}, {"key": "C", "text": "f(x) = (x + 1)(x − 2)"}, {"key": "D", "text": "f(x) = (x − 2)(x + 1)²\n\nAbdelrahman Ghoneem | 01116004434 | EST & SAT Math Page 15"}], "correct": "A", "explanation": "Verified answer key; source and release checks completed."}, "source_question_id": "ghoneem_full_revision_jun_2026-q00071", "source": "Matching original question and graph: Clean bank PAM 008 page 312 / HOA 008 page 7; full revision page 15 Q72", "review_note": "Restored the exact source graph from the matching original question; independently checked its roots and multiplicities.", "release": false, "fields": {"stem": "Which function is represented by the graph?", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "\\(f(x)=(x+1)(x-2)^2\\)"}, {"key": "B", "text": "\\(f(x)=(x-1)(x+2)^2\\)"}, {"key": "C", "text": "\\(f(x)=(x+1)(x-2)\\)"}, {"key": "D", "text": "\\(f(x)=(x-2)(x+1)^2\\)"}]}, "explanation": "The graph crosses the x-axis at −1, so (x + 1) is a factor of odd multiplicity. It touches and turns at x = 2, so (x − 2) is squared. It rises to the right and passes through (0, 4), all consistent with (x + 1)(x − 2)².", "correct": "A", "figure_from_id": "2e905225-ae8b-63b0-1de2-dbd31cbbca60", "duplicate_of": "2e905225-ae8b-63b0-1de2-dbd31cbbca60"}, {"id": "0403c781-b2bf-bebd-b514-e18502c670b4", "before": {"stem": "7.\n In the figure above .Find the length of CD", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "11.3"}, {"key": "B", "text": "18.6"}, {"key": "C", "text": "1.7"}, {"key": "D", "text": "1.3\n\n 2"}], "correct": "A", "explanation": "In the left 45-45-90 right triangle, AB = 18 gives AC = 18/sqrt(2) = 9sqrt(2). In the right triangle ACD, CD = AC sin(63 degrees) about 11.3."}, "source_question_id": "est1_dec_2025-q00007", "source": "est1_dec_2025; source page 2", "review_note": "Removed page-footer contamination; independently solved the complete problem and checked every choice.", "release": true, "fields": {"choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "11.3"}, {"key": "B", "text": "18.6"}, {"key": "C", "text": "1.7"}, {"key": "D", "text": "1.3"}]}, "explanation": "Triangle ABC is a 45–45–90 right triangle with hypotenuse AB = 18, so AC = 18/√2 = 9√2. In right triangle ACD, CD = AC sin 63° = 9√2 sin 63° ≈ 11.3.", "correct": "A"}]$payload$::jsonb) LOOP
