function notation by the equivalent listed-preimage question; solved both roots and removed following section text.", "release": true, "fields": {"choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "6"}, {"key": "B", "text": "−6"}, {"key": "C", "text": "−1"}, {"key": "D", "text": "0"}], "stem": "If g(x) = 2x² + 10x − 10, which listed value of x satisfies g(x) = 2?"}, "explanation": "Solve g(x) = 2: 2x² + 10x − 12 = 0, or (x + 6)(x − 1) = 0. The inputs are −6 and 1; only −6 is listed. This asks for a preimage and does not assume an unrestricted quadratic has an inverse function.", "correct": "B"}, {"id": "e5e6a8d9-4714-b889-0a39-60babf2cb117", "before": {"stem": "If g(x) = 2x² + 10x − 10, find a value of g⁻¹(2).", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "6"}, {"key": "B", "text": "−6"}, {"key": "C", "text": "−1"}, {"key": "D", "text": "0\nSECTION 6 — January 2026 — Official-Style Questions\nKEY IDEAS\n• Read charts before computing: list the data values, then apply the formula.\n• Best-fit line: slope = rise/run between trend points; intercept = predicted y at x = 0.\n• Prediction: substitute the x-value into the best-fit equation."}], "correct": "B", "explanation": "Answer key transcribed from the complete printed source key and structurally validated against the listed choices."}, "source_question_id": "full_subject_revision_june_2026-p43-q199", "source": "full_subject_revision_june_2026; source page 43", "review_note": "Replaced ambiguous inverse-function notation by the equivalent listed-preimage question; solved both roots and removed following section text.", "release": true, "fields": {"choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "6"}, {"key": "B", "text": "−6"}, {"key": "C", "text": "−1"}, {"key": "D", "text": "0"}], "stem": "If g(x) = 2x² + 10x − 10, which listed value of x satisfies g(x) = 2?"}, "explanation": "Solve g(x) = 2: 2x² + 10x − 12 = 0, or (x + 6)(x − 1) = 0. The inputs are −6 and 1; only −6 is listed. This asks for a preimage and does not assume an unrestricted quadratic has an inverse function.", "correct": "B"}, {"id": "280f30f4-dfb6-aaaa-f667-e269590b90fa", "before": {"stem": "Q143. What is the IQR of 4, 6, 7, 8, 10, 12, 20?", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "6"}, {"key": "B", "text": "8"}, {"key": "C", "text": "12"}, {"key": "D", "text": "16\n\nAbdelrahman Ghoneem | 01116004434 | EST & SAT Math Page 28"}], "correct": "A", "explanation": "Verified answer key; source and release checks completed."}, "source_question_id": "ghoneem_full_revision_jun_2026-q00139", "source": "ghoneem_full_revision_jun_2026; source page 28", "review_note": "Removed page-footer contamination; independently solved the complete problem and checked every choice.", "release": true, "fields": {"choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "6"}, {"key": "B", "text": "8"}, {"key": "C", "text": "12"}, {"key": "D", "text": "16"}], "stem": "For the data 4, 6, 7, 8, 10, 12, 20, what is the interquartile range? Use the medians of the lower and upper halves, excluding the overall median."}, "explanation": "Exclude the overall median 8. The lower half is 4, 6, 7, so Q1 = 6. The upper half is 10, 12, 20, so Q3 = 12. The IQR is 12 − 6 = 6.", "correct": "A"}, {"id": "3199bc92-dd9b-9023-525b-0fc410124380", "before": {"stem": "Question 25. In the figure above, the curve 𝐶𝑓 represents the graph of a function 𝑓. Which of the\n following rational functions correspond to 𝑓?\n\n 1−𝑥2", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "(𝑥+1)(𝑥−3)\n\n 𝑥2−1"}, {"key": "B", "text": "(𝑥+1)(𝑥−3)\n\n 𝑥2−4"}, {"key": "C", "text": "(𝑥+1)(𝑥−3)\n\n 4−𝑥2"}, {"key": "D", "text": "(𝑥−1)(𝑥−3)\n\n 𝑥2−4"}, {"key": "E", "text": "(−𝑥+1)(𝑥+3)\n\n 10\nESTII-HS Math L1 January 2026"}], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The graph has vertical asymptotes x=-1 and x=3 and zeros x=-2 and x=2. The matching rational function is (x^2-4)/((x+1)(x-3)), choice C."}, "source_question_id": "est2_hs_level1_jan_2026-q00025", "source": "est2_hs_level1_jan_2026; source page 10", "review_note": "January 2026 physical page 10 visually checked: restored each numerator and denominator and supplied the graph features explicitly.", "release": true, "fields": {"choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "(1 − x²)/((x + 1)(x − 3))"}, {"key": "B", "text": "(x² − 1)/((x + 1)(x − 3))"}, {"key": "C", "text": "(x² − 4)/((x + 1)(x − 3))"}, {"key": "D", "text": "(4 − x²)/((x − 1)(x − 3))"}, {"key": "E", "text": "(x² − 4)/((1 − x)(x + 3))"}], "stem": "The graph has vertical asymptotes x = −1 and x = 3, x-intercepts −2 and 2, and horizontal asymptote y = 1. Which rational function matches it?"}, "explanation": "The graph has vertical asymptotes x = −1 and x = 3, so its denominator contains (x + 1)(x − 3). Its x-intercepts are −2 and 2, so its numerator is x² − 4. The leading-coefficient ratio is 1, matching the horizontal asymptote y = 1. Only C has all these features.", "correct": "C"}, {"id": "a86f7c30-0a2d-6ab8-f945-6e633e7ece98", "before": {"stem": "Question 25. In the figure above, the curve 𝐶𝑓 represents the graph of a function 𝑓. Which of the\n following rational functions correspond to 𝑓?\n\n 1−𝑥2", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "(𝑥+1)(𝑥−3)\n\n 𝑥2−1"}, {"key": "B", "text": "(𝑥+1)(𝑥−3)\n\n 𝑥2−4"}, {"key": "C", "text": "(𝑥+1)(𝑥−3)\n\n 4−𝑥2"}, {"key": "D", "text": "(𝑥−1)(𝑥−3)\n\n 𝑥2−4"}, {"key": "E", "text": "(−𝑥+1)(𝑥+3)\n\n 10\nESTII-HS Math L1 January 2026"}], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The graph has vertical asymptotes x=-1 and x=3 and zeros x=-2 and x=2. The matching rational function is (x^2-4)/((x+1)(x-3)), choice C."}, "source_question_id": "est2_hs_level1_jan_2026-q00285", "source": "est2_hs_level1_jan_2026; source page 175", "review_note": "January 2026 physical page 10 visually checked: restored each numerator and denominator and supplied the graph features explicitly.", "release": true, "fields": {"choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "(1 − x²)/((x + 1)(x − 3))"}, {"key": "B", "text": "(x² − 1)/((x + 1)(x − 3))"}, {"key": "C", "text": "(x² − 4)/((x + 1)(x − 3))"}, {"key": "D", "text": "(4 − x²)/((x − 1)(x − 3))"}, {"key": "E", "text": "(x² − 4)/((1 − x)(x + 3))"}], "stem": "The graph has vertical asymptotes x = −1 and x = 3, x-intercepts −2 and 2, and horizontal asymptote y = 1. Which rational function matches it?"}, "explanation": "The graph has vertical asymptotes x = −1 and x = 3, so its denominator contains (x + 1)(x − 3). Its x-intercepts are −2 and 2, so its numerator is x² − 4. The leading-coefficient ratio is 1, matching the horizontal asymptote y = 1. Only C has all these features.", "correct": "C"}]$payload$::jsonb) LOOP
