w_note": "Removed page-footer contamination; independently solved the complete problem and checked every choice.", "release": true, "fields": {"choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "(0, 0)"}, {"key": "B", "text": "(1, 1)"}, {"key": "C", "text": "(1, 2)"}, {"key": "D", "text": "(2, 3)"}, {"key": "E", "text": "(0, 3)"}], "stem": "Which point does not satisfy both y ≥ 2x − 1 and y < −x + 4?"}, "explanation": "For (2,3), the first inequality holds: 3 ≥ 2(2) − 1 = 3. The second fails: 3 is not less than −2 + 4 = 2. Each other listed point satisfies both inequalities, so D is the only excluded point.", "correct": "D"}, {"id": "e47fd9cf-67df-5724-0285-08517803a48e", "before": {"stem": "4. How many books did he originally have?", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "32"}, {"key": "B", "text": "34"}, {"key": "C", "text": "36"}, {"key": "D", "text": "40\n\nAbdelrahman Ghoneem | 01116004434 | EST & SAT Math Page 36"}], "correct": "D", "explanation": "Verified answer key; source and release checks completed."}, "source_question_id": "ghoneem_full_revision_jun_2026-q00160", "source": "ghoneem_full_revision_jun_2026; source page 36", "review_note": "Recovered the missing opening sentence from original page 36 Q11; solved the equation and checked the integer counts.", "release": true, "fields": {"choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "32"}, {"key": "B", "text": "34"}, {"key": "C", "text": "36"}, {"key": "D", "text": "40"}], "stem": "Abdo threw away 10% of his books, gave his sister 12 books, donated 20 books, and kept 4. How many books did he originally have?"}, "explanation": "Let the original number be n. After discarding 10%, Abdo has 0.9n books. Giving away 12 + 20 and keeping 4 gives 0.9n − 32 = 4. Thus 0.9n = 36 and n = 40. Check: discard 4, then 40 − 4 − 12 − 20 = 4.", "correct": "D"}, {"id": "e56b843a-4f6c-9e96-eea3-e313c15d343e", "before": {"stem": "Q44. Given i = √(−1), (2i + 5)(i − 1) =", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "−7 + 3i"}, {"key": "B", "text": "−7 − 3i"}, {"key": "C", "text": "−3 + 3i"}, {"key": "D", "text": "3 + 3i\n\nAbdelrahman Ghoneem | 01116004434 | EST & SAT Math Page 42"}], "correct": "A", "explanation": "Verified answer key; source and release checks completed."}, "source_question_id": "ghoneem_full_revision_jun_2026-q00191", "source": "ghoneem_full_revision_jun_2026; source page 42", "review_note": "Removed page-footer contamination; independently solved the complete problem and checked every choice.", "release": true, "fields": {"choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "−7 + 3i"}, {"key": "B", "text": "−7 − 3i"}, {"key": "C", "text": "−3 + 3i"}, {"key": "D", "text": "3 + 3i"}]}, "explanation": "Expand: (2i + 5)(i − 1) = 2i² − 2i + 5i − 5. With i² = −1, this is −2 + 3i − 5 = −7 + 3i.", "correct": "A"}, {"id": "ec632604-76ad-3ad6-714d-e6653d7a7f64", "before": {"stem": "Q77. Identify the leading coefficient, degree, and end behavior of P(x) = −5x⁴ −\n 6x² + 6.", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "Leading coefficient −5, degree 4; as x → −∞, P(x) → +6 and as x → +∞, P(x) → +6"}, {"key": "B", "text": "Leading coefficient −5, degree 6; as x → −∞, P(x) → −∞ and as x → +∞, P(x) → −∞"}, {"key": "C", "text": "Leading coefficient −5, degree 6; as x → −∞, P(x) → +6 and as x → +∞, P(x) → +6"}, {"key": "D", "text": "Leading coefficient −5, degree 4; as x → −∞, P(x) → −∞ and as x → +∞, P(x) → −∞\n\nAbdelrahman Ghoneem | 01116004434 | EST & SAT Math Page 16"}], "correct": "D", "explanation": "Verified answer key; source and release checks completed."}, "source_question_id": "ghoneem_full_revision_jun_2026-q00076", "source": "ghoneem_full_revision_jun_2026; source page 16", "review_note": "Removed page-footer contamination; independently solved the complete problem and checked every choice.", "release": true, "fields": {"choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "Leading coefficient −5, degree 4; as x → −∞, P(x) → +6 and as x → +∞, P(x) → +6"}, {"key": "B", "text": "Leading coefficient −5, degree 6; as x → −∞, P(x) → −∞ and as x → +∞, P(x) → −∞"}, {"key": "C", "text": "Leading coefficient −5, degree 6; as x → −∞, P(x) → +6 and as x → +∞, P(x) → +6"}, {"key": "D", "text": "Leading coefficient −5, degree 4; as x → −∞, P(x) → −∞ and as x → +∞, P(x) → −∞"}]}, "explanation": "The highest-degree term is −5x⁴, so the leading coefficient is −5 and the degree is 4. As x tends to either positive or negative infinity, x⁴ tends to positive infinity and −5x⁴ dominates, so P(x) tends to negative infinity at both ends.", "correct": "D"}, {"id": "ee6df2bd-4492-6d26-5793-5f7fa29340a1", "before": {"stem": "48. If 2x-1=4 , find the value of x+1", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "5/2"}, {"key": "B", "text": "5"}, {"key": "C", "text": "3"}, {"key": "D", "text": "7/2\n\n 12"}], "correct": "D", "explanation": "2x - 1 = 4 gives x = 5/2, so x + 1 = 7/2."}, "source_question_id": "est1_dec_2025-q00044", "source": "est1_dec_2025; source page 12", "review_note": "Removed page-footer contamination; independently solved the complete problem and checked every choice.", "release": true, "fields": {"choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "5/2"}, {"key": "B", "text": "5"}, {"key": "C", "text": "3"}, {"key": "D", "text": "7/2"}]}, "explanation": "From 2x − 1 = 4, obtain 2x = 5 and x = 5/2. Then x + 1 = 5/2 + 2/2 = 7/2.", "correct": "D"}]$payload$::jsonb) LOOP
